usb: phy: generic: do not demand a VBUS regulator for a node-less device

usb_phy_gen_create_phy() asks for the "vbus" supply with an exclusive
get.  When the device came from the device tree that is right: the
phandle either resolves or it genuinely is not there.  When the device
was built in code it can never resolve, because there is no node to hold
a vbus-supply property -- and usb_phy_generic_register() builds exactly
such a device.  drivers/usb/musb/mediatek.c calls it for every MUSB
glue, MT6592's included.

The regulator core answers an unresolvable lookup by offering its dummy
regulator, then declines to hand a dummy to an exclusive request and
says so:

  usb_phy_generic usb_phy_generic.2.auto: dummy supplies not allowed for
  exclusive requests (id=vbus)

The three lines below the get already read that -ENODEV as "none fitted,
carry on", so the message announces a condition the caller has handled.
It is emitted at KERN_WARNING, which on the J36 Ultra means it is printed
to the console -- and the console on this board is the panel, mid-splash,
where the whole point is that nothing is printed at all.

Gate the get on dev->of_node, which is the same test this function
already uses a few lines up for the clock and reset properties.  Devices
that do come from a node keep their vbus-supply and every error path that
went with it.

Signed-off-by: MixOS J36 Ultra bring-up
---
diff --git a/drivers/usb/phy/phy-generic.c b/drivers/usb/phy/phy-generic.c
--- a/drivers/usb/phy/phy-generic.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/phy/phy-generic.c
@@ -256,12 +256,30 @@
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(nop->vcc),
 				     "could not get vcc regulator\n");
 
-	nop->vbus_draw = devm_regulator_get_exclusive(dev, "vbus");
-	if (PTR_ERR(nop->vbus_draw) == -ENODEV)
-		nop->vbus_draw = NULL;
-	if (IS_ERR(nop->vbus_draw))
-		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(nop->vbus_draw),
-				     "could not get vbus regulator\n");
+	/*
+	 * Only ask for a VBUS regulator when there is a node that could have
+	 * named one.  usb_phy_generic_register() builds this device in code --
+	 * drivers/usb/musb/mediatek.c does it for every MUSB glue, MT6592's
+	 * included -- so it has no of_node, no vbus-supply phandle and nothing
+	 * for the regulator core to resolve.  The core offers its dummy, will
+	 * not give a dummy to an exclusive request, and warns:
+	 *
+	 *   usb_phy_generic usb_phy_generic.2.auto: dummy supplies not allowed
+	 *   for exclusive requests (id=vbus)
+	 *
+	 * The code below already reads that -ENODEV as "none fitted, carry on",
+	 * so the warning announces a handled condition -- at KERN_WARNING, which
+	 * on this board puts it on the panel in the middle of the boot splash.
+	 * A device that does come from a node is unaffected.
+	 */
+	if (dev->of_node) {
+		nop->vbus_draw = devm_regulator_get_exclusive(dev, "vbus");
+		if (PTR_ERR(nop->vbus_draw) == -ENODEV)
+			nop->vbus_draw = NULL;
+		if (IS_ERR(nop->vbus_draw))
+			return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(nop->vbus_draw),
+					     "could not get vbus regulator\n");
+	}
 
 	nop->dev		= dev;
 	nop->phy.dev		= nop->dev;
